To manually consume a WCF service using HttpClient in .NET Core 8, follow these steps:

Scenario:

Assume you are consuming a WCF service that has a GetCustomer method, exposed at:

http://localhost:5000/CustomerService.svc


---

1. Set Up the HttpClient

Install the latest System.Net.Http package (if not already included):

dotnet add package System.Net.Http


---

2. Create a HttpClient Service

Use HttpClient to send requests to the WCF endpoint.

using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Text;
using System.Text.Json;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class CustomerServiceClient
{
    private readonly HttpClient _httpClient;
    private readonly string _serviceUrl = "http://localhost:5000/CustomerService.svc";

    public CustomerServiceClient(HttpClient httpClient)
    {
        _httpClient = httpClient;
    }

    public async Task<string> GetCustomerAsync(int customerId)
    {
        try
        {
            string url = $"{_serviceUrl}/GetCustomer";
            
            // Create the SOAP request
            var soapRequest = $@"<?xml version=""1.0"" encoding=""utf-8""?>
            <soap:Envelope xmlns:xsi=""http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"" 
                           xmlns:xsd=""http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"" 
                           xmlns:soap=""http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/envelope/"">
              <soap:Body>
                <GetCustomer xmlns=""http://tempuri.org/"">
                  <customerId>{customerId}</customerId>
                </GetCustomer>
              </soap:Body>
            </soap:Envelope>";

            var content = new StringContent(soapRequest, Encoding.UTF8, "text/xml");

            // Send the request
            HttpResponseMessage response = await _httpClient.PostAsync(url, content);
            response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();

            string responseBody = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
            return responseBody;
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            return $"Error: {ex.Message}";
        }
    }
}


---

3. Consume the Service in a Console App

In Program.cs, instantiate the HttpClient and call the WCF method:

using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class Program
{
    static async Task Main(string[] args)
    {
        var httpClient = new HttpClient();
        var client = new CustomerServiceClient(httpClient);
        
        var result = await client.GetCustomerAsync(123);
        Console.WriteLine(result);
    }
}


---

4. Handle SOAP Action (Optional for Some Services)

Some WCF services may require a SOAPAction in the header:

content.Headers.Add("SOAPAction", "http://tempuri.org/ICustomerService/GetCustomer");


---

5. Parse the Response

If the response is XML, you can parse it using XmlDocument or XDocument:

using System.Xml.Linq;

XDocument doc = XDocument.Parse(responseBody);
string customerName = doc.Descendants("CustomerName").FirstOrDefault()?.Value;
Console.WriteLine($"Customer Name: {customerName}");


---

Key Points:

SOAP Envelope: WCF uses SOAP envelopes, so structure the request in XML format.

URL Encoding: Ensure special characters in XML are properly escaped.

Error Handling: Catch HTTP errors and handle timeout scenarios.

Deserialization: If the service returns complex objects, deserialize the response into C# objects using XmlSerializer or JsonSerializer.



